I'm sure I previously found something which said that the regulation was 
being applied to sterling/euro transactions. The Euro legislation leaves it 
open for some of the provisions to be applied to Swedish, Danish and Uk 
currency at the respective governments' discretion. What I can't remember 
was whether that application to sterling was a general thing or just my 
bank (HSBC).

Ah, I think http://www.euro.gov.uk/crossborder.asp explains it.

I think it says that so long as the order is given in euro, the euro 
regulations apply. If the order is given in pounds they do not. So 
transferring euro to a uk account is covered, transferring pounds to a non-
uk account is not.

Also, you must use the IBAN and BIC codes for this to apply. Your bank 
should be able to tell you your account number in IBAN format, or it may be 
on the statements. e.g. read this (randomly googled) discussion: 
